%N A400558 Table read by antidiagonals: T(n,m) = Zarankiewicz number z(n,m;2,2) (n >= 2, m >= 2).
%C A400558 The Zarankiewicz number z(n,m;s,t) is the largest r such that some n X m binary matrix with r ones does not contain a s X t submatrix of ones.  The Zarankiewicz problem asks for a formula for the Zarankiewicz numbers.  The table (read by antidiagonals) gives z(n,m;2,2).
%C A400558 Equivalently, z(n,m;2,2) is the maximum possible number of edges in a bipartite graph with part sizes n and m which does not contain K_{2,2} (a 4-cycle) as a subgraph.
%C A400558 Equivalently, z(n,m;2,2) is the maximum possible sum of numbers of vertices of cliques in a packing of m cliques into K_n.  This can be seen from the clique-vertex incidence matrix, as two cliques cannot overlap on two vertices.
%D A400558 Donald E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, Vol. 4B: Combinatorial Algorithms, Part 2, Addison-Wesley, 2023, section 7.2.2.2, pp. 289-291.
%H A400558 Allan Bickle, <a href="https://allanbickle.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ngmultipaper-1.pdf">Nordhaus-Gaddum Theorems for Multifactor Decompositions</a>, Springer PROMS 462 (2022) 297-303.
%H A400558 Gábor Damásdi, Tamás Heger, and Tamás Szőnyi, <a href="https://heger.web.elte.hu/publ/Damasdi-Heger-Szonyi-Zarankiewicz-cages-geometries.pdf">The Zarankiewicz problem, cages, and geometries</a>, Annales Univ. Eötvös Loránd LVI, 3-37 (2013).
%H A400558 Z. Furedi, A. Kostochka, M. Stiebitz, R. Skrekovski, and D. West, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.20113">Nordhaus-Gaddum-type theorems for decompositions into many parts</a>, J. Graph Theory 50 (2005), 273-292.
%H A400558 R. K. Guy, <a href="/A001197/a001197.pdf">A problem of Zarankiewicz</a>, Research Paper No. 12, Dept. of Math., Univ. Calgary, Jan. 1967. [Annotated and scanned copy, with permission]
%H A400558 R. K. Guy, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0060112">A many-facetted problem of Zarankiewicz</a>, Lect. Notes Math. 110 (1969), 129-148.
%H A400558 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zarankiewicz_problem">Zarankiewicz problem</a>.
%F A400558 T(n,m) = T(m,n).
%F A400558 T(n,m) = m + C(n,2) for m >= C(n,2).
%F A400558 a(n) = A376167(n) - 1.
%F A400558 T(n,n) <= (1 + sqrt(4*n-3))*n/2.
